  "findings": [
    {
      "anchor": "docs/plans/the-monitor-reads-each-fact-once.html:899 (s104)",
      "id": "done_when-1",
      "reason": "the done-when must either give the counted requests live pointers so the pass does not settle them, or state that a record this pass itself settled is not recounted, otherwise the node is dispatched against an assertion no correct implementation can meet.",
      "text": "the fixture's failed and waiting requests are settled by the read itself: `failure_obligations` calls `_settle` for a request whose run has no live pointer (reckon/crew/promotion_requests.py:1421-1427), and `_settle` rewrites the request file (promotion_requests.py:1364-1375). Reproduced over a temporary crew home with one failed, one waiting and one already-settled request: the first `failure_obligations(\"p1\",\"s1\")` call returned no duties and rewrote both undecided files (`rewritten_on_first_reads: True`, phases became `promotion-settled gone`); a second call returned no duties and rewrote nothing. So with any cache keyed on the file's identity, each settled request is decoded again by the next session in the same publish (its stat moved under the first session's write), and the second publish decodes it again too \u2014 the assertions \"decodes each request at most once\" and \"a second publish with no file changed decodes none\" fail on a correct implementation",
      "type": "done_when",
      "would_change": true
    },
    {
      "anchor": "docs/plans/the-monitor-reads-each-fact-once.html:899 (s104)",
      "id": "reasoning-1",
      "reason": "the reuse map belongs to the node brief; with it in the section the plan cannot be answered by a different seam that also reads each request once, and the sentence is a guess a reviewer has to test against the code.",
      "text": "the Deliverable prescribes the mechanism, \"reuse the `file_memo` machinery s96 uses for pointers\", naming the module to extend rather than the record the publish reads",
      "type": "reasoning",
      "would_change": true
    },
    {
      "anchor": "docs/plans/the-monitor-reads-each-fact-once.html:903 (s105)",
      "id": "reasoning-2",
      "reason": "the endpoint is the count scanned once per unchanged log pair; naming the internal seam as the counted stand-in binds the implementation to one private function and belongs in the brief.",
      "text": "the done-when fixes the seam by counting calls to the private `_gate_log_format` through a stand-in, so the section names a function to call and extend rather than a quantity to measure",
      "type": "reasoning",
      "would_change": true
    }
  ],
